# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in real values.
# `.env` is gitignored; NEVER commit real values. Perms should be 0600.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THERE ARE THREE VARIABLES, AND THIS IS NO LONGER WHERE THE BRIDGE IS
# CONFIGURED
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Since 2026-08-04 (ADR 0023) the CONFIGURATION IS `config.toml`, in the state
# directory, edited from the web UI. Eleven variables that used to live here —
# the Sparkplug identity, the broker, the meter, the publish period, the log
# settings — were withdrawn. Setting them now does nothing at all.
#
# The environment keeps exactly what cannot be in that file:
#
#   * the smart-me credential, which never descends to disk;
#   * SMARTME_STATE_DIR, because a file cannot say where it lives.
#
# The two sources have no field in common. That is deliberate: any overlap would
# need a precedence rule, and a precedence rule is a place where one source loses
# without saying so — a value changed in the browser reverting at the next
# restart to a stale line in this file, with no error and no trace.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# smart-me API credentials — REQUIRED, and a PAIR
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OAuth2 Client Credentials grant ("Device" app type; smart-me's recommended
# replacement for Basic auth on embedded devices). The bridge exchanges these for
# a bearer token, then calls the API with `Authorization: Bearer <token>`.
#
# Both halves live here, together, and that is a decision rather than an
# oversight (ADR 0023 §3). `client_id` is not itself sensitive and could have
# gone in the file — but a credential split across two sources can be rotated by
# halves, and a mismatched pair fails as an authentication rejection from the
# smart-me API. That reads as an OUTAGE of the upstream service rather than as a
# configuration fault, and gets diagnosed accordingly, at length.
SMARTME_CLIENT_ID=
SMARTME_CLIENT_SECRET=
#
# FALLBACK — HTTP Basic auth (smart-me account user/password). RESERVED, and NOT
# read by the bridge today: `smart-me-client` implements Basic (ADR 0009) but the
# binary builds client-credentials unconditionally and never constructs it, so
# these two have no effect. And the pair above is REQUIRED, so setting these
# *instead* of it makes the bridge refuse to start.
# SMARTME_BASIC_USER=
# SMARTME_BASIC_PASSWORD=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# State directory
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Holds `config.toml` and the persisted session number (`bdseq.toml`). Defaults
# to /data, which is the container mount point.
#
# WITH NO config.toml IN IT, the bridge still starts: it comes up, serves the web
# UI, and publishes nothing — no CONNECT, no NBIRTH, nothing on the broker at
# all — until a configuration exists. That is the first run, and it is not an
# error. A configuration that EXISTS and is invalid is a different matter: the
# bridge refuses to start and lists every fault at once.
# SMARTME_STATE_DIR=/data

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Log level
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Standard `tracing` filter, applied to the console and the file alike. Defaults
# to INFO — deliberately, and NOT to the ERROR that the library's own default
# would give, which would hide the ignored-NCMD traces, the QoS-downgrade warning
# and every traced drop. Raise it to debug one subsystem without drowning:
# RUST_LOG=info,smartme_bridge::adapters::mqtt_driver=debug
# RUST_LOG=info
#
# NOTE: RUST_LOG stays here and is NOT a setting in config.toml. It has to be
# readable before the configuration is, since raising the level is what one does
# to diagnose a configuration that will not load.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT MOVED INTO config.toml
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Set from the web UI, or written by hand for a headless bring-up. The file
# carries a schema version and refuses anything it does not recognise, rather
# than letting an unknown key take its default:
#
#   schema_version       = 4
#   group_id             Sparkplug group. REQUIRED, no default — a host PERSISTS
#                        what it discovers, so the wrong namespace leaves a tag
#                        folder that outlives the process and must be deleted by
#                        hand. It defaulted to `Site` until 2026-07-31, while the
#                        Tier-3 contract test had always refused to publish into
#                        a group by that name: the guard was on the disposable
#                        artifact and missing from the real one.
#   node_id              Sparkplug edge node. REQUIRED, same reason.
#   broker_host          Host or IP of the MQTT broker. REQUIRED.
#   broker_port          Defaults to 1883.
#   publish_period_secs  Bounded, min 5, max 300, default 30, and there is
#                        deliberately no value meaning "off" (ADR 0020). The
#                        maximum is not a preference: the periodic publish is
#                        what lets a host that restarted notice a node whose
#                        BIRTH it never saw and ask for a rebirth (ADR 0018).
#   api_base             Optional. Must be https; a plaintext base is refused.
#   log_dir              Optional. Setting it turns FILE logging on — the file is
#                        `smartme_mqtt.<YYYY-MM-DD>.log`, rotated daily. Unset
#                        means console only, which is what the chaos tests that
#                        spawn the real binary depend on.
#   log_keep             Rotated files kept. Defaults to 7 (one week).
#   mapping_confirmed    Whether a human has checked the meter->topic mapping.
#                        ABSENT MEANS FALSE, and NOTHING is published until it is
#                        true — no connection, no birth. Editing the mapping
#                        withdraws it automatically. Writing the file by hand and
#                        setting it counts as confirming it: typing the mapping
#                        out is having looked at it.
#   ui_port              Port the embedded web UI listens on INSIDE the
#                        container. Defaults to 8080 — a default is required
#                        rather than convenient, because the first run has no
#                        file to read a port from and is the run that most needs
#                        a screen. The container publishes no host port: Traefik
#                        reaches it over a shared Docker network and is the only
#                        thing that can (ADR 0019).
#   [[meters]]           meter_id, device_id, serial, enabled — one table per
#                        meter. The MODEL holds any number; the RUNTIME serves
#                        one today, and enabling more is refused rather than
#                        silently truncated.
#
# Broker authentication (FR43, NFR16) is still RESERVED and not honoured. When it
# lands, the password will follow the credential discipline above — environment
# only — and be enabled together with broker TLS, since MQTT sends credentials in
# the clear inside the CONNECT packet.
